message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyunte (동현테): a fixed-view single-player tennis laboratory configured through Donghyun-lang"
type: software
version: "0.08"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24133576"
date-released: "2026-11-12"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Raspberry-Kim/donghyun-lab18"
url: "https://raspberry-kim.github.io/donghyun-lab18/"
abstract: >-
  Donghyunte is a browser-based single-player singles tennis game. Programs
  written in Donghyun-lang select one of seven players, three difficulties,
  and four courts. Keyboard and standard-mapped gamepads control play; mouse
  and touch do not control the match. The fixed camera keeps the player at
  the bottom even after a change of ends. The game includes a two-stage serve,
  differentiated strokes and lobs, fourteen special abilities, deuce,
  tiebreaks, and best-of-three sets. A match in progress can be saved to
  three browser slots or a .dhsave file and restored exactly. Game Boy Tennis (1989) was run and observed
  as a reference; its machine code was not reverse-engineered, and the physics
  constants and hit rules of this independent implementation do not
  numerically match the original. In-app "Why Open?" and "Mathematical
  principles" pages explain the history of open tennis and the engine's
  equations. Version 0.08 enlarges the character heads, offers 63 examples,
  and makes sustained abilities last for the rally while unused one-shot
  winning abilities carry forward until a successful landing. The opponent
  AI never uses special abilities. The 0.12-second post-hit serve input
  window and court coefficients are original gameplay adjustments. No ROM,
  emulator state, original-game screenshot, extracted art, or original audio
  is distributed. Code and documentation are MIT; bundled fonts are OFL.
keywords:
  - tennis
  - browser game
  - esoteric programming language
  - JavaScript
  - gamepad
